"""Additional equation, model-domain and result-acceptance corrections."""
from revise_foundations_scientific_text import revise,BOOK,ledger
import re

def sub(ch,id,pattern,new,issue,sources=None):
 if any(r['id']==id for r in ledger):return
 path=next((BOOK/'chapters').glob(ch+'*/chapter.md'))
 match=re.search(pattern,path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'),re.S)
 if not match:raise ValueError(id)
 revise(ch,id,match[0],new,issue,sources or ['analytical derivation; current NeqSim implementation'])

sub('ch03','ch03_dry_domain',r'\| Dry gas \| > 100,000 \| — \| — \| No liquid at any conditions \|','| Dry gas | > 100,000 | — | — | No hydrocarbon liquid along the stated reservoir-to-surface path |','Dry-gas classification does not prohibit condensation at arbitrary cryogenic conditions.')
sub('ch03','ch03_sampling_temperature',r'Opening pressure of the sample bottle should match.*?gas loss\.', 'Opening pressure must be compared at a specified temperature after thermal equilibration and with the sampling pressure/volume history accounted for. A cooler bottle can have lower pressure without leaking; unexpected pressure loss at matched conditions warrants a leak or gas-loss investigation.','Sampling pressure QC omitted temperature and bottle-volume history.')
sub('ch03','ch03_cvd_z',r'\*\*Two-phase Z-factor\*\*:.*?reservoirs',r'**Two-phase Z-factor**: $Z_{\mathrm{2ph},j}=P_j V_{\mathrm{cell}}/(n_{\mathrm{remaining},j}RT)$, with $n_{\mathrm{remaining},j}=n_0-\sum_{k\leq j}\Delta n_k$. This is a bulk inventory factor, distinct from the gas-phase Z-factor. Its use in a reservoir material balance must track composition-dependent withdrawal and condensate inventory','CVD two-phase Z incorrectly multiplied gas Z by remaining mole fraction.')
sub('ch03','ch03_pedersen_density',r'\\rho_n = 0\.2855 \+ 0\.0326 \\cdot \\ln\(n\)',r'\rho_n = A_\rho+B_\rho\ln(n)','Removed unphysical universal density coefficients; coefficients must match measured fractions.')
sub('ch03','ch03_pedersen_basis',r'The Pedersen method is particularly useful',r'Here $M_n$ is in g/mol and denotes a petroleum pseudo-component estimate, not the exact molar mass of an n-alkane ($14n+2$ approximately). Fit $A_\rho$ and $B_\rho$ in a declared density unit to the measured fractions and plus-fraction density; require positive, plausible extrapolated densities. The Pedersen method is particularly useful','Defined fitted density and pseudo-component molecular-weight basis.')
sub('ch03','ch03_volume_translation',r'Cubic equations of state \(SRK, PR\) systematically underpredict liquid density by 5–15%\. The Peneloux volume translation corrects this without affecting the phase equilibrium calculation:',r'Cubic EOS liquid-density bias depends on fluid, state and parameterization; neither its sign nor a fixed percentage is universal. The consistent Péneloux translation can improve volumes while preserving the original phase-equilibrium conditions.\cite{foundationPeneloux1982} For a translation with the stated sign convention:', 'Removed unsupported universal cubic-EOS error and qualified the VLE-preserving translation.', ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0378381282800022'])
sub('ch03','ch03_fvf_standard_z',r'B_g = \\frac\{ZT P\^\{\\text\{sc\}\}\}\{T\^\{\\text\{sc\}\} P\}',r'B_g = \frac{ZT P^{\text{sc}}}{Z^{\text{sc}}T^{\text{sc}} P}','Gas formation-volume factor omitted standard-state compressibility.')
sub('ch03','ch03_viscosity_units',r'\*\*Dead oil viscosity\*\* \(Beggs-Robinson\):','**Dead oil viscosity** (Beggs–Robinson; viscosity in cP, temperature $T$ in °F, within the correlation calibration domain):','Empirical viscosity equation lacked required temperature and viscosity units.')
sub('ch03','ch03_mass_vs_normalization',r'\*\*Mass balance:\*\* Verify that the sum of all mole fractions equals 1\.0','**Composition normalization and conservation:** Verify mole fractions sum to1, then separately check total moles, mass and heavy-fraction molecular weight before and after characterization. Normalization alone is not a mass balance.','Normalized composition was incorrectly called a mass-balance check.')
sub('ch03','ch03_grading_dimensionless',r'\\ln f_i\(T, P\(z\), x_i\(z\)\) = \\ln f_i\(T, P_\{\\text\{ref\}\}, x_\{i,\\text\{ref\}\}\) \+ \\frac\{M_i g \(z - z_\{\\text\{ref\}\}\)\}\{RT\}',r'\ln\!\left[\frac{f_i(T,P(z),\mathbf{x}(z))}{f_i(T,P_{\mathrm{ref}},\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{ref}})}\right]=\frac{M_i g(z-z_{\mathrm{ref}})}{RT}','Fugacity ratio is dimensionless; fugacity depends on full composition vector.')
sub('ch03','ch03_grading_domain',r'where \$f_i\$ is the fugacity of component \$i\$ and \$z\$ is depth\.',r'Here $z$ increases downward, $M_i$ is kg/mol, and the column is isothermal and in gravitational equilibrium. A geothermal gradient requires a non-isothermal grading model; substituting a varying temperature into this integrated equation is not sufficient.','Defined gravity sign and isothermal grading assumption.')
sub('ch04','ch04_radial_boundary',r'For practical field units \(bbl/day, mD, ft, psi, cp\):',r'For pseudo-steady flow in a closed circular drainage area, use average reservoir pressure $\bar P$ rather than the boundary pressure $P_e$. In field units (STB/day, mD, ft, psi, cP):','Steady boundary-pressure equation was conflated with pseudo-steady average-pressure equation.')
sub('ch04','ch04_radial_field_pressure',r'q_o = \\frac\{0\.00708\\, k h \(P_e - P_\{wf\}\)\}',r'q_o = \frac{0.00708\, k h (\bar P - P_{wf})}','Correct pressure basis for the -0.75 pseudo-steady constant.')
sub('ch04','ch04_theis_euler',r'- 2\\gamma \+ 2S',r'- \gamma + 2S','Line-source exponential-integral asymptote has -Euler gamma, not -2 gamma.', ['https://www.opengeosys.org/6.5.7/docs/benchmarks/liquid-flow/liquid-flow-theis-problem/'])
sub('ch04','ch04_afterflow_direction',r'Fluid stored in the wellbore continues to flow into the formation after surface shut-in', 'Formation fluid continues to enter and compress the wellbore inventory after surface shut-in of a producer','Production afterflow direction was reversed.')
sub('ch04','ch04_slope_sign',r'where \$m\$ is the slope of the Horner straight line\.', 'Here $m$ is the positive magnitude of the Horner straight-line slope; the plotted pressure decreases with increasing logarithmic Horner ratio.','Permeability requires positive slope magnitude.')
sub('ch04','ch04_undersaturated_balance',r'\*\*Volumetric undersaturated oil\*\*.*?\$F = N E_\{fw\}\$',r'**Volumetric undersaturated oil** ($m=0$, $W_e=0$, above $P_b$): $F=N(E_o+E_{fw})$, where $E_o=B_o-B_{oi}$; oil expansion must be retained','Undersaturated oil material balance omitted oil expansion.')
sub('ch04','ch04_gascap_compressibility_general',r'N\\frac\{B_\{oi\}\(c_w S_\{wi\}\+c_f\)\}',r'N\frac{(1+m)B_{oi}(c_w S_{wi}+c_f)}','Total reservoir rock/connate-water expansion must scale with initial oil plus gas-cap pore volume.')
sub('ch04','ch04_gascap_compressibility_ho',r'E_\{fw\} = \\frac\{B_\{oi\}\(c_w S_\{wi\} \+ c_f\)\}',r'E_{fw} = \frac{(1+m)B_{oi}(c_w S_{wi} + c_f)}','Aligned Havlena–Odeh compressibility term with whole-tank volume.')
sub('ch04','ch04_aquifer_si',r'U = \\frac\{2\\pi f \\phi_a c_t h_a r_a\^2\}\{5\.615\}',r'U = 2\pi f\phi_a c_t h_a r_a^2','Removed unexplained field-unit conversion from otherwise SI aquifer formulation.')
sub('ch04','ch04_aquifer_units',r'where \$f\$ is the fraction of the aquifer circle',r'Use SI consistently: permeability $k_a$ in m², time in s, viscosity in Pa s, compressibility in Pa$^{-1}$, and lengths in m; $U$ then has units m³/Pa. The symbol $k_a$ here is permeability, distinct from the preceding steady-influx coefficient. Here $f$ is the fraction of the aquifer circle','Specified aquifer coefficient dimensions and distinguished overloaded symbols.')
sub('ch04','ch04_reserves_categories',r'- \*\*P90 \(Proved\):\*\*.*?optimistic case',r'- **P90 / low estimate:** at least90% probability of equaling or exceeding the stated recoverable quantity; corresponds to1P (Proved) when all reserves criteria are met.\n- **P50 / best estimate:** at least50% exceedance probability; corresponds to2P (Proved plus Probable), not Probable alone.\n- **P10 / high estimate:** at least10% exceedance probability; corresponds to3P (Proved plus Probable plus Possible), not Possible alone.\n\nA simulated quantity distribution does not establish commerciality or reserves status. Apply the complete PRMS project and economic criteria.\cite{foundationPRMS2018}'.replace('\\n','\n'),'Corrected incremental versus cumulative reserves categories.', ['https://www.spe.org/en/industry/petroleum-resources-management-system-2018/'])
sub('ch05','ch05_pressure_sign',r'\\frac\{dP\}\{dz\} = \\underbrace',r'-\frac{dP}{ds} = \underbrace','Positive upward-loss equation needs negative pressure gradient along flow.')
sub('ch05','ch05_pressure_derivative_coordinate',r'\\frac\{dv_m\}\{dz\}',r'\frac{dv_m}{ds}','Matched acceleration derivative to positive flow-distance coordinate.')
sub('ch05','ch05_gradient_basis',r'- \$\\rho_m\$ = mixture density \[kg/m³\]',r'- $s$ = distance along the upward flow path [m]; $\theta>0$ uphill; $f$ is the Darcy friction factor. The displayed expression is a homogeneous-mixture balance. Slip correlations use holdup density for gravity and their own friction/acceleration closures, not one interchangeable density for every term.\n- $\rho_m$ = homogeneous-mixture density [kg/m³]'.replace('\\n','\n'),'Distinguished homogeneous momentum equation from slip-model closures.')
sub('ch05','ch05_jt_sign',r'\\Delta T = \\mu_\{JT\} \\cdot \\Delta P',r'T_2-T_1 \simeq \mu_{JT}(P_2-P_1)','JT cooling requires consistent signed pressure change.')
sub('ch05','ch05_jt_domain',r'The pressure reduction across the choke is isenthalpic \(constant enthalpy\)\. For gases, this results in cooling due to the Joule-Thomson effect:',r'An adiabatic valve with negligible net kinetic/potential-energy change is isenthalpic. A positive Joule–Thomson coefficient gives cooling during pressure reduction; the coefficient can change sign. The following local linear estimate is suitable only for a small pressure interval; use a PH flash over a large letdown:','Qualified isenthalpic and small-interval JT approximations.')
sub('ch05','ch05_choke_flow_basis',r'where \$C_d\$ is the discharge coefficient.*?choke type\)\.',r'Here $q_g$ is actual volumetric flow at the upstream state in m³/s, not standard gas volume; $P_1$ is absolute Pa, $\rho_1$ is kg/m³ and $A$ is m². This ideal-gas, constant-$\gamma$ nozzle relation applies above the critical downstream/upstream pressure ratio. Below it, evaluate the bracket at the critical ratio; mass flow then remains capacity-limited. Real control valves require tested flow coefficients and expansion/recovery factors.','Prevented applying subcritical nozzle equation below critical ratio or as standard volume.')
sub('ch05','ch05_gaslift_optimum',r'This economic optimum is always less than the technical maximum rate\.',r'For a smooth concave response with positive marginal injection cost and an interior optimum, the economic injection rate is below the rate-maximizing injection. Bound-active wells or nonconcave responses require explicit constrained optimization; equal slopes apply only to unconstrained wells.','Economic-optimum and equal-slope statements require KKT/domain conditions.')
sub('ch05','ch05_esp_fixedrate',r'ESPs are essentially fixed-rate devices — changing the rate requires a variable speed drive \(VSD\)',r'At fixed speed an ESP operates at the intersection of its head–flow curve with the system curve; changing backpressure changes rate. A variable-speed drive provides an additional control variable','Centrifugal ESP incorrectly described as a fixed-rate device.')
sub('ch05','ch05_vfp_monotonic',r'\*\*Table consistency:\*\* The VFP table must be monotonic.*?liquid-loading region\.',r'**Table consistency:** A physical multiphase VFP curve can be non-monotonic: decreasing hydrostatic head at low rate competes with increasing friction at high rate. Do not force monotonicity to hide this behavior. Check each branch for hydraulic convergence, units and interpolation error; assess dynamic stability separately before choosing an operating point.','Removed false mandatory monotonicity that could erase physical low-rate branches.')
sub('ch05','ch05_skin_numeric',r'each unit of skin consumes approximately 0\.7 bar of bottomhole pressure\.',r'each unit of skin consumes $141.2(5000)B/10000=70.6B$ psi, or $4.87B$ bar, with $B$ in reservoir bbl/STB. For $B=1.2$, this is5.84bar per skin unit.','Corrected numerical skin-pressure estimate by nearly an order of magnitude.')
sub('ch06','ch06_speed_intro',r'which implements a Newton-Raphson Global Gradient Algorithm \(NR-GGA\) solver capable of handling hundreds of wells with sub-second convergence\.',r'which implements a Newton–Raphson Global Gradient Algorithm (NR-GGA). Runtime and convergence must be measured for the actual topology, fluid, closures and starting point.','Removed unbenchmarked subsecond performance guarantee.')
sub('ch06','ch06_schur_sign',r'= r_2 - A_\{21\} A_\{11\}\^\{-1\} r_1',r'= A_{21}A_{11}^{-1}r_1-r_2','Corrected Schur-complement right-hand-side sign from displayed block equations.')
sub('ch06','ch06_esp_head',r'\\Delta P_\{ESP\} = \\rho \\cdot g \\cdot H_\{stage\} \\cdot N_\{stages\} \\cdot \\eta',r'\Delta P_{ESP}=\rho g H_{stage}N_{stages},\qquad P_{shaft}=Q_{vol}\Delta P_{ESP}/\eta','Hydraulic head was incorrectly multiplied by pump efficiency.')
sub('ch06','ch06_summary_speed',r'\*\*The NR-GGA solver\*\*.*?uncertainty analysis\.',r'**The NR-GGA solver** reduces the network system through a Schur complement. Accept a candidate only after checking hydraulic residuals, nodal mass balance and every element’s supported model status; runtime alone does not establish suitability.','Summary repeated unsupported speed guarantee.')
sub('ch06','ch06_gaslift_proxy',r'\*\*Artificial lift\*\* \(gas lift, ESP, jet pump, rod pump\) is fully integrated.*?settings\.',r'**Artificial lift switches** provide screening pressure boosts. The current network gas-lift switch reduces computed loss using a capped gas/produced-mass ratio; it does not inject a material stream or predict the full hydrostatic/friction optimum. Use the explicit lift-gas mixer and tubing workflow of Chapter5 for component and energy balances.','Current gas-lift pressure proxy was falsely described as a complete coupled lift model.', ['source: LoopedPipeNetwork.calculateHeadLoss artificial lift section'])

